{
  "case_id": "2021-21-441",
  "document_hash": "d43168ea462a3a142b9fb28e1a3be537a6e08ce881fe6ef12ab3dfe3fa40feba",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Daniel L. Geyser",
      "case_id": "2021-21-441",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d43168ea462a3a142b9fb28e1a3be537a6e08ce881fe6ef12ab3dfe3fa40feba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d43168ea462a-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GEYSER: Thank you, Mr. Chief\nJustice, and may it please the Court:\nThe 2017 Act's quarterly fee increase\npresents a clear and obvious violation of the\nBankruptcy Clause's uniformity requirement.\nCongress arbitrarily divided the country into\ntwo different groups and then authorized\ndifferent fees for identically situated debtors\nbecause their bankruptcies happened to arise in\ndifferent states.\nThere are no regional differences,\ndistinct local conditions, or industry-specific\nproblems justifying this non-uniform treatment.\nThe division is entirely artificial. There's\nnothing unique about North Carolina or Alabama\nthat justifies a separate bankruptcy system with\nits own special lower fees. Congress has simply"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2021-21-441",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d43168ea462a3a142b9fb28e1a3be537a6e08ce881fe6ef12ab3dfe3fa40feba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d43168ea462a-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Mr. Geyser, is the\nreal problem here as to lack of uniformity the\nfees, the differential fees, or the original\ndivision of the country into two different types\nof districts?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2021-21-441",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d43168ea462a3a142b9fb28e1a3be537a6e08ce881fe6ef12ab3dfe3fa40feba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d43168ea462a-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Well, but I --\nI think it -- one answer, ready answer, is,\nwell, they're treating them differently because"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2021-21-441",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d43168ea462a3a142b9fb28e1a3be537a6e08ce881fe6ef12ab3dfe3fa40feba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d43168ea462a-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: I'm not -- I'm not --\nI understand my example is not perfect. What\nI'd like to know is, what case have you found\nthat in your opinion goes the farthest in saying\nsomething is non-uniform in the bankruptcy area\nand, therefore, unconstitutional?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2021-21-441",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d43168ea462a3a142b9fb28e1a3be537a6e08ce881fe6ef12ab3dfe3fa40feba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d43168ea462a-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: What -- what\nexactly was the concern at the time of the\nframing that led the framers to put this clause\nin the Constitution?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2021-21-441",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d43168ea462a3a142b9fb28e1a3be537a6e08ce881fe6ef12ab3dfe3fa40feba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d43168ea462a-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Counsel, I -- I --\nI'm having a difficulty because you're trying to\nestablish a broad rule in a situation that I\ndon't think lends itself to it given our case\nlaw."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2021-21-441",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d43168ea462a3a142b9fb28e1a3be537a6e08ce881fe6ef12ab3dfe3fa40feba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d43168ea462a-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: What legal standard do\nyou think governs this issue of leveling up or\nleveling down, as Justice Sotomayor put it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Geyser",
      "case_id": "2021-21-441",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "d43168ea462a3a142b9fb28e1a3be537a6e08ce881fe6ef12ab3dfe3fa40feba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d43168ea462a-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But -- but, yeah, I don't think there's anything that prevents this Court from saying we objected, we have fees that we would like back, we have an open case. And the proper constitutional remedy is to equalize the treatment by having us pay the lower fees. And any other debtor has to litigate on -- on their own terms based on their own procedural posture."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Geyser",
      "case_id": "2021-21-441",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "d43168ea462a3a142b9fb28e1a3be537a6e08ce881fe6ef12ab3dfe3fa40feba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d43168ea462a-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We hope you do agree with us on the merits. I think, if the Court would like to remand to the lower courts to sort out the remedy question, that's certainly an option."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2021-21-441",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "d43168ea462a3a142b9fb28e1a3be537a6e08ce881fe6ef12ab3dfe3fa40feba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d43168ea462a-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Those examples that you give of, say, ERISA, or what are your federal tax obligations, those aren't plausible exercises of the bankruptcy power, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2021-21-441",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "d43168ea462a3a142b9fb28e1a3be537a6e08ce881fe6ef12ab3dfe3fa40feba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d43168ea462a-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This, the facial uniformity requirement applies, in your view, only to what you're describing as substantive bankruptcy regulations like priority for creditors?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2021-21-441",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d43168ea462a3a142b9fb28e1a3be537a6e08ce881fe6ef12ab3dfe3fa40feba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d43168ea462a-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 70,
        "start": 70
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GANNON: But the $324 million figure is calculated by figuring out which debtors in the U.S. Trustee program districts paid the heightened fee that was associated with at least a million dollars of disbursements in a quarter for any of the 13 quarters --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-21-441",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "d43168ea462a3a142b9fb28e1a3be537a6e08ce881fe6ef12ab3dfe3fa40feba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d43168ea462a-0004-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 87,
        "start": 87
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Congress used the word \"may\" in contrast to the word \"shall\" in the very interlocking provision that they were dealing with and then followed it up in (a)(7) with the next sentence that uses the word \"shall\" twice."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-21-441",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "d43168ea462a3a142b9fb28e1a3be537a6e08ce881fe6ef12ab3dfe3fa40feba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d43168ea462a-0004-0001",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 90,
        "start": 90
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
